		<entry>
		<author><name><![CDATA[KD7MW]]></name></author>
		<updated>2024-07-03T08:02:54</updated>

		<published>2024-07-03T08:02:54</published>
		<id>http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4158&amp;p=31909#p31909</id>
		<link href="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4158&amp;p=31909#p31909"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: MD-9600 Receive Audio, how to improve?]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4158&amp;p=31909#p31909"><![CDATA[
Thanks, Roger. I added the L-Pad to the Motorola speaker, and it did indeed help. I dialed in just enough attenuation so the DMR audio's usable range was a little higher on the volume control. Now I have a much more usable setup, and it's far less hair-trigger than before. I am already using the AGC +0 setting. <br><br>On my 9600, analog FM audio comes up gradually as the volume control is advanced. But before adding the L-Pad, DMR audio came on suddenly, medium-loud, and a couple of degrees higher, it was painfully loud. Even now, for a given volume setting, DMR is always substantially louder. <br><br>I also discovered that the external speaker jack is not well-made. Just from inserting and removing a standard 3mm plug a few times, the internal speaker is now always muted. The contacts that disconnect the internal speaker appear to be bent enough that they no longer make contact when the plug is removed. And the connector is sealed in black plastic, so bending the contacts back into position won't be easy. I don't want to risk destroying the jack entirely, so I may just leave it alone. Since I want to use the Motorola speaker anyway, it's probably not worth disassembling the whole radio and removing the main board to replace the jack.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=2447">KD7MW</a> — Wed Jul 03, 2024 8:02 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
