		<author><name><![CDATA[KD7MW]]></name></author>
		<updated>2024-06-27T22:41:01</updated>

		<published>2024-06-27T22:41:01</published>
		<id>http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4158&amp;p=31852#p31852</id>
		<link href="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4158&amp;p=31852#p31852"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: MD-9600 Receive Audio, how to improve?]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4158&amp;p=31852#p31852"><![CDATA[
The Motorola HSN4038A speaker is a vast improvement over both the internal speaker and the MFJ “Cleartone” speaker. The audio frequency response is “just right” for my ears. All other speakers were either too “tubby” sounding, or would overly accentuate the DMR-typical distortion of higher-frequency consonants, which was driving me crazy.<br><br>I ordered an inexpensive variable L-pad from Amazon. I’ll see if that can reduce the audio at the speaker sufficiently that a reasonable average volume is beyond what is now the “suddenly too loud” point on the radio’s volume control. Even with the added resistor mod, it goes from too soft to too loud in just a couple of degrees at around the 8:00 o’clock position. Interestingly, this is not an issue on analog FM receive. <br><br>I’ve also noticed that even with the hardware click suppression mod, I am still getting some loud clicks sometimes. Adding the firmware click suppression as well often seems to help.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=2447">KD7MW</a> — Thu Jun 27, 2024 10:41 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
		<entry>
		<author><name><![CDATA[KD7MW]]></name></author>
		<updated>2024-06-23T08:18:41</updated>

		<published>2024-06-23T08:18:41</published>
		<id>http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4158&amp;p=31744#p31744</id>
		<link href="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4158&amp;p=31744#p31744"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: MD-9600 Receive Audio, how to improve?]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4158&amp;p=31744#p31744"><![CDATA[
I have an MFJ "ClearTone" speaker. It works very well for CW on HF. I tried it with the 9600, but wasn't completely happy with it. Very tinny-sounding.  It might very well be a good mobile speaker, as the high frequencies would carry over road noise. What I want for the shack is a reasonable amount of midtones and a little extra treble for intelligibility.<br><br>Being a major big spender, I decided to risk $20 (including shipping)  <img class="smilies" src="http://localhost/opengd77/images/smilies/icon_e_wink.gif" width="15" height="17" alt=";)" title="Wink">  and ordered a used Motorola HSN4038A external speaker from The Auction Site. It has a reputation for very good audio. If anybody knows how to handle DMR audio, it ought to be Motorola, right?  I should get it early next week. Stay tuned, I'll let you all know how it works out.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=2447">KD7MW</a> — Sun Jun 23, 2024 8:18 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
